# Authorization Sequence (mandatory)

Applies to: every Next.js Server Action, API Route Handler (app/api),
and background job (queue worker, cron, scheduled task). Do not skip
or reorder these steps for any code that touches tenant/org data.

## Trigger detection
Before writing any handler, confirm whether it touches org-scoped data.
If yes, the full sequence below is mandatory. If a step cannot be
satisfied, STOP and surface the gap instead of proceeding.

## Required sequence (in order)
1. Authenticate the caller.
   - Server Action / Route: read the Supabase auth session or Clerk
     session from the request context (server-side only — never trust
     client-supplied user/org ids).
2. Resolve the active organization from a server-validated source:
   the auth session claim, or an org id derived from the URL/route
   context (e.g. /orgs/[orgId]/...). URL org ids must be validated
   against the authenticated user's memberships.
3. Confirm the user belongs to that organization (membership row exists
   and is not revoked/expired).
4. Confirm the user's role is sufficient for the action (e.g. owner,
   admin, member) per a defined role table — never hardcode role
   checks inline; reference the canonical role list.
5. Confirm the organization has the required entitlement/plan for the
   action (feature flag, plan tier, or quota check).
6. Execute the operation using RLS-protected database access only.
   All queries must go through a Supabase client bound to the
   authenticated user (set role / org context) so RLS enforces isolation.
   No service-role queries that bypass RLS in user-facing handlers.
7. Create an audit event for sensitive operations (write/mutation,
   billing change, role/permission change, data export). Audit events
   must be written in the same transaction or immediately after,
   capturing: user id, org id, action, target, timestamp, outcome.

## Hard constraints
- Never execute the operation (step 6) before steps 1–5 pass.
- Never accept org id, user id, or role from the client request body
  as authoritative — treat as hints to be validated server-side.
- If existing code does not follow this sequence, refactor it to match
  before adding new functionality on top of it. Flag deviations in your
  response rather than silently leaving them.
- Service-role (bypass-RLS) access is allowed only in admin/background
  jobs with an explicit, audited org context — never in user request paths.